  "measured_on": "2026-08-04",
  "why": "Seventeen sentences in English, French and Arabic, plus one in each that must route to nothing — 54 cases (#354). Seven are single-pillar, one per registered pillar, so no pillar is scored on another's evidence. Ten are mixed, and each is a way the split can go wrong rather than another compound sentence: a quantity sitting beside the pillar it does not belong to, one time governing two clauses and stated once, two pillars sharing one object, a clause that belongs to nothing beside one that does, and three pillars at once. The same expectations are asserted in all three languages because meaning travels as an id: 'cardio' is 'cardio' whatever the sentence was in, and a case that scored in English only proved the router understands English. Nothing asserts free text — `item` is stored in the user's own words (D79), so 'eggs' and 'two eggs' are both right and a case naming one asserts a phrasing. Fields nobody asserts are not scored: a null the spec permits is not an error, and neither is a judgement call — eggs are 'meat' to the model and 'other' to the person who wrote the case, and the vocabulary supports both. The one thing deliberately left unasserted is the clock on 'this morning I ran and ate two eggs': the model answers 09:00:00 exactly, which is an invention rather than something the sentence said, and pinning either that or its absence would be asserting a preference. It is reported on #354 instead.",
